From: StanleyYP Wang <StanleyYP.Wang@mediatek.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Feb 2024 17:46:10 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: mac80211: run the DFS CAC after the CSA countdown

cfg80211 rejects a CSA to a DFS channel that still needs a CAC. An AP
that must leave a radar channel therefore cannot move to such a
channel with a CSA.

mac80211 now completes the countdown, runs the CAC on the new channel
and finalizes the switch when the CAC ends. Beaconing stays off during
the CAC, so that a later CSA does not reuse a stale CSA beacon. An end
of the CAC from cfg80211 also finalizes the switch. mac80211 then acts
only on the interface that cfg80211 names, because cfg80211 clears the
CAC state of that interface only. A radar on the new channel during
the countdown stops the AP, because a CAC would end the non-occupancy
period early. A radar during the CAC cancels it, allows a new CSA and
tells the driver to end the quiet period of the switch. A new CSA
during the CAC cancels only the CAC of its own link, because hostapd
sends one request per BSS. The new CSA cancels that CAC before the new
switch, because the driver must end the old switch first. If that CSA
fails, the link stops, because its channel has no CAC. A switch that
fails to finalize at once returns the error, so that nl80211 can undo
a skipped CAC. A background chain that overlaps the new channel stops
when the CAC starts, and the channel that the AP leaves keeps its two
second grace.

Signed-off-by: StanleyYP Wang <StanleyYP.Wang@mediatek.com>
Co-developed-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
